-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 - the provisions of Chapters 1 to 3 and the sub-provisions elsewhere that
-- the first pass left `inert` and that the completion pass of 2026-11-04 moved out of `inert`, because
-- the reasons given for leaving them out ("a convention", "carried in the @desc of a leaf", "addresses
-- an interpretive canon", "no leaf asks it", "another law's question") are not reasons the brief allows.
-- By one Sonnet 5.5 agent.
-- Source: Penal Code 1871, 2020 Revised Edition, SSO "Current version as at 09 Sep 2026" (a converter's
-- plain-text rendering, not the official text), registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.
-- Each quotation below is a `-- src:N | text` line copied from that file by generators/deferred/expand.py;
-- each `@ref` carries the locator of its lines; `python3 -I srcrefs.py check --refs <raw> <this file>`
-- reports 0 problems.
--
-- What a reader must know before reading:
--
-- * Everything here is a function over a small typed representation, or over a few BOOLEAN inputs, that
--   returns MAYBE BOOLEAN or a MAYBE of a named type.  JUST FALSE only where the text says no in its own
--   words: a "means" definition; an "if", "unless", "except" or "other than" condition; a flat negative
--   ("is not necessary", "is not a defence", "does not need").  Everything else - "includes", "denotes",
--   "refers to", a grant or rule limited by its own wording, a question the text does not answer - is
--   NOTHING, or a named "not covered" constructor.  Every such branch carries a `-- FALSE-kind` or a
--   `-- NOTHING-kind` comment, and NOTES.md has "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING".
-- * A rule of reading ("every definition is understood subject to the exceptions", "the expression is used
--   in conformity with the explanation") is a function whose inputs say which case is before it, tested on
--   both sides of each limb.  A definition is a function with a test per entry.
-- * A fact that belongs to another Act (the Electronic Transactions Act 2010's "electronic record"; the
--   law that makes a person "liable by law to be tried" for an act beyond Singapore) is an input, never a
--   guess.
-- * `s 1` (short title) and `s 4B(3)` (the Minister may amend the Schedule by order) are not here: they stay
--   `inert`, as machinery (the brief's reason 2).
-- * Where the first-pass modules still say that these provisions are "inert" (pc-general.l4, near its
--   top; pc-general-part.l4 at s 108B), those comments are stale and were not edited (NOTES.md says so).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pc-domain`
IMPORT `pc-general`

§ `Penal Code 1871 - provisions moved out of inert (completion pass 2026-11-04)`

§§ `s 2 - punishment of offences committed within Singapore`

-- src:1240 | Punishment of offences committed within Singapore
-- src:1242 | 2. Every person shall be liable to punishment under this Code and not otherwise for
-- src:1243 | every act or omission contrary to the provisions thereof, of which he is guilty within
-- src:1244 | Singapore.
-- "Every person shall be liable to punishment under this Code and not otherwise for every act or omission contrary to the provisions
-- thereof, of which he is guilty within Singapore."  Two answers: the liability under this Code, which the text states for acts of which the
-- person is guilty within Singapore, and the "not otherwise", the text's own no to liability under any other law for those acts (FORK DEF-M1).
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 2 (src:1240-1244)
@export Whether a person is liable to punishment under the Penal Code 1871 (section 2): every person is, for every act or omission contrary to the provisions of the Code of which he is guilty within Singapore; NOTHING where the act or omission is not one of which he is guilty within Singapore, which section 2 does not speak to (sections 3 to 4B and others extend the Code)
GIVEN `the person is guilty within Singapore of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of this Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person guilty, within Singapore, of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of the Penal Code 1871?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2 the person is liable to punishment under this Code` MEANS
    IF `the person is guilty within Singapore of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of this Code`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 2 says who is liable for acts of which he is guilty within Singapore; it does not say that a person who is not so guilty is not liable under the Code (ss 3, 4, 4A and 4B extend it)

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 2 (src:1240-1244)
@export Whether a person is liable to punishment otherwise than under the Penal Code 1871 for an act or omission contrary to its provisions of which he is guilty within Singapore (section 2: "and not otherwise"); NOTHING where he is not so guilty
GIVEN `the person is guilty within Singapore of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of this Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person guilty, within Singapore, of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of the Penal Code 1871?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2 the person is liable to punishment otherwise than under this Code` MEANS
    IF `the person is guilty within Singapore of an act or omission contrary to the provisions of this Code`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Every person shall be liable to punishment under this Code and not otherwise for every act or omission contrary to the provisions thereof, of which he is guilty within Singapore"
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): section 2 speaks only of acts or omissions of which the person is guilty within Singapore

§§ `s 3 - offences committed beyond, but which by law may be tried within, Singapore`

-- src:1246 | Punishment of offences committed beyond, but which by law may be tried within
-- src:1247 | Singapore
-- src:1249 | 3. Any person liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of
-- src:1250 | Singapore, shall be dealt with according to the provisions of this Code for any act
-- src:1251 | committed beyond Singapore, in the same manner as if such act had been committed
-- src:1252 | within Singapore.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 3 (src:1246-1252)
DECLARE `Where an act is done` IS ONE OF
    `within Singapore`
    `beyond the limits of Singapore`

-- "Any person liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of Singapore" is a fact of the law that makes him liable to be tried
-- (the Criminal Procedure Code 2010, the Extradition Act and others, none in the sources): an input.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 3 (src:1246-1252)
@export Where an act is to be taken to have been done for the purposes of the Penal Code 1871 (section 3): a person liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of Singapore is dealt with under the Code for the act in the same manner as if it had been committed within Singapore; NOTHING where the section does not speak
GIVEN `where the act was done` IS A `Where an act is done` @desc Where was the act in fact done?
      `the person is liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of Singapore? That liability comes from a law that is not in the sources: the investigator answers it
GIVETH A MAYBE `Where an act is done`
`s 3 where the act is taken to have been done` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is liable by law to be tried for an offence committed beyond the limits of Singapore` AND `where the act was done` EQUALS `beyond the limits of Singapore`
        THEN JUST `within Singapore`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 3 treats an act committed beyond Singapore, by a person liable by law to be tried for it, as if committed within Singapore; it says nothing of an act done within Singapore, nor of a person who is not so liable

§§ `s 5 - certain laws not to be affected by this Code`

-- src:1309 | Certain laws not to be affected by this Code
-- src:1311 | 5. Nothing in this Code is intended to repeal, vary, suspend, or affect any of the
-- src:1312 | provisions of any Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the
-- src:1313 | Singapore Armed Forces, or of any other law for the time being in force.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 5 (src:1309-1313)
@export Whether the Penal Code 1871 repeals, varies, suspends or affects a law (section 5: nothing in the Code is intended to do so to any Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the Singapore Armed Forces, or any other law for the time being in force); NOTHING where the law is not one in force
GIVEN `the law is an Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the Singapore Armed Forces, or any other law for the time being in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the law an Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the Singapore Armed Forces, or any other law for the time being in force?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5 this Code affects the law` MEANS
    IF `the law is an Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the Singapore Armed Forces, or any other law for the time being in force`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Code is intended to repeal, vary, suspend, or affect any of the provisions of any Act for punishing mutiny and desertion of officers or servicemen in the Singapore Armed Forces, or of any other law for the time being in force" (a flat negative)
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): section 5 speaks only of laws in force; whether the Code affects a law no longer in force it does not say

§§ `s 6 - definitions in this Code to be understood subject to exceptions`

-- src:1319 | Definitions in this Code to be understood subject to exceptions
-- src:1321 | 6. Throughout this Code every definition of an offence, every penal provision, and
-- src:1322 | every illustration of every such definition or penal provision, shall be understood subject
-- src:1323 | to the exceptions contained in the Chapters entitled “General Exceptions” and “Right of
-- src:1324 | Private Defence”, though those exceptions are not repeated in such definition, penal
-- src:1325 | provision or illustration.
-- src:1327 | Illustrations
-- src:1329 | (a) The sections in this Code which contain definitions of offences, do not express, that a child
-- src:1330 | below 10 years of age cannot commit such offences, but the definitions are to be understood subject to
-- src:1331 | the general exception which provides that “nothing shall be an offence which is done by a child below
-- src:1332 | 10 years of age”.
-- src:1334 | (b) A, a police officer, without warrant, apprehends Z, who has committed murder. Here A is not
-- src:1335 | guilty of the offence of wrongful confinement, for he was bound by law to apprehend Z, and therefore
-- src:1336 | the case falls within the general exception which provides that “nothing is an offence which is done by
-- src:1337 | a person who is bound by law to do it”.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 6 (src:1319-1337)
DECLARE `Kind of provision` IS ONE OF
    `a definition of an offence`
    `a penal provision`
    `an illustration of a definition of an offence or of a penal provision`
    `a provision of another kind`

-- Illustration (a): the definitions of offences do not say that a child below 10 years of age cannot commit them, but are understood subject to the general
-- exception that "nothing shall be an offence which is done by a child below 10 years of age" (s 82, in pc-exceptions-general.l4).
-- Illustration (b): A, a police officer, apprehends Z, who has committed murder, without a warrant: not guilty of wrongful confinement, for he was bound by law to
-- apprehend Z (s 76, in pc-exceptions-general.l4).  Both are tested in pc-general-moved-tests.l4.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 6 (src:1319-1337)
@export Whether a provision of the Penal Code 1871 is to be understood subject to the exceptions in the Chapters entitled "General Exceptions" and "Right of Private Defence" (section 6): every definition of an offence, every penal provision, and every illustration of either is, though the exceptions are not repeated; NOTHING for any other kind of provision
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of provision` @desc What kind of provision of the Code is it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6 the provision is understood subject to the General Exceptions and the Right of Private Defence` MEANS
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `a definition of an offence` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a penal provision` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `an illustration of a definition of an offence or of a penal provision` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a provision of another kind` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 6 names three kinds of provision; it does not say that a provision of another kind (a definition of a word, for example) is not understood subject to the exceptions

§§ `s 6A - definitions to apply to this Code and other written law`

-- src:1341 | Definitions to apply to this Code and other written law
-- src:1343 | 6A. Every definition of a word or expression which is explained in sections 22A to
-- src:1344 | 26H (except the definitions of “dishonestly” and “fraudulently” in sections 24 and 25,
-- src:1345 | respectively) applies to any offence in this Code or in any other written law unless that
-- src:1346 | written law expressly provides for a definition or explanation of that same word or
-- src:1347 | expression.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 6A (src:1341-1347)
@export Whether a definition applies to an offence under section 6A of the Penal Code 1871: every definition of a word or expression explained in sections 22A to 26H (except those of "dishonestly" in section 24 and "fraudulently" in section 25) applies to any offence in the Code or in any other written law, unless that written law expressly provides for a definition or explanation of the same word or expression; NOTHING for a definition outside sections 22A to 26H
GIVEN `the definition is explained in sections 22A to 26H` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the definition one that is explained in sections 22A to 26H?
      `the definition is that of "dishonestly" in section 24 or of "fraudulently" in section 25` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the definition of "dishonestly" in section 24, or of "fraudulently" in section 25?
      `the offence is in this Code or in any other written law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one in the Penal Code 1871 or in any other written law?
      `that written law expressly provides for a definition or explanation of that same word or expression` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the written law the offence is in expressly provide for a definition or explanation of that same word or expression?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6A the definition applies to the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the definition is explained in sections 22A to 26H`)
        THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 6A speaks only of the definitions explained in sections 22A to 26H
        IF NOT (`the offence is in this Code or in any other written law`)
        THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): an offence in neither the Code nor any other written law is not one section 6A speaks to
        IF `the definition is that of "dishonestly" in section 24 or of "fraudulently" in section 25`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(except the definitions of \"dishonestly\" and \"fraudulently\" in sections 24 and 25, respectively)"
        IF `that written law expressly provides for a definition or explanation of that same word or expression`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless that written law expressly provides for a definition or explanation of that same word or expression"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 7 - expression once explained is used in the same sense throughout this Code`

-- src:1351 | Expression once explained is used in the same sense throughout this Code
-- src:1353 | 7. Every expression which is explained in any part of this Code is used in every part
-- src:1354 | of this Code in conformity with the explanation.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 7 (src:1351-1354)
@export Whether an expression is used in conformity with its explanation (section 7 of the Penal Code 1871): every expression explained in any part of the Code is used in every part of the Code in conformity with the explanation; NOTHING where the expression is not explained in the Code or not used in the Code
GIVEN `the expression is explained in a part of this Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the expression explained in some part of the Penal Code 1871?
      `the expression is used in a part of this Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the expression used in a part of the Penal Code 1871?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7 the expression is used in conformity with its explanation` MEANS
    IF `the expression is explained in a part of this Code` AND `the expression is used in a part of this Code`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 7 speaks of an expression explained in the Code and used in the Code; it says nothing of one that is not

§§ `s 8 - "gender"`

-- src:1356 | “Gender”
-- src:1358 | 8. The pronoun “he” and its derivatives are used of any person, whether male or
-- src:1359 | female.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 8 (src:1356-1359)
@export Whether a pronoun is used of a person under section 8 of the Penal Code 1871: the pronoun "he" and its derivatives are used of any person, whether male or female; NOTHING for any other pronoun
GIVEN pronoun IS A Pronoun @desc The pronoun the Code uses
      sex IS A Sex @desc The sex of the person it is used of
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 8 the pronoun is used of the person` MEANS
    IF pronoun EQUALS he AND (sex EQUALS male OR sex EQUALS female)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 8 speaks of "he" and its derivatives; it says nothing of "she" or "they"

§§ `s 9 - "number"`

-- src:1361 | “Number”
-- src:1363 | 9. Unless the contrary appears from the context, words importing the singular
-- src:1364 | number include the plural number, and words importing the plural number include the
-- src:1365 | singular number.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 9 (src:1361-1365)
DECLARE `Grammatical number` IS ONE OF
    singular
    plural

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 9 (src:1361-1365)
@export The other grammatical number that words importing the given number include under section 9 of the Penal Code 1871 (words importing the singular include the plural, and words importing the plural include the singular)
GIVEN n IS A `Grammatical number` @desc The number the words import
GIVETH A `Grammatical number`
`s 9 the other number` MEANS
    CONSIDER n
    WHEN singular THEN plural
    WHEN plural THEN singular

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 9 (src:1361-1365)
@export Whether words importing one number include the other under section 9 of the Penal Code 1871: they do, unless the contrary appears from the context
GIVEN `the contrary appears from the context` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contrary appear from the context?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9 the words include the other number` MEANS
    IF `the contrary appears from the context`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Unless the contrary appears from the context, words importing the singular number include the plural number, and words importing the plural number include the singular number"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 12 and 17 - "public" and "Government"`

-- src:1378 | “Public”
-- src:1379 | 12. The word “public” includes any class of the public or any community.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 12 (src:1378-1379)
@export Whether a group of persons is "the public" under section 12 of the Penal Code 1871: the word includes any class of the public or any community; NOTHING for any other group (the list is not closed)
GIVEN `it is a class of the public or a community` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the group a class of the public, or a community?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12 the group is the public` MEANS
    IF `it is a class of the public or a community`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "The word \"public\" includes any class of the public or any community": a group that is neither is not said to be outside the word

-- src:1383 | “Government”
-- src:1384 | 17. The word “Government” includes any person lawfully performing executive
-- src:1386 | functions of the Government under any law.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 17 (src:1383-1386)
@export Whether a person is "the Government" under section 17 of the Penal Code 1871: the word includes any person lawfully performing executive functions of the Government under any law; NOTHING for any other person (the list is not closed)
GIVEN `the person is lawfully performing executive functions of the Government under a law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person lawfully performing executive functions of the Government under any law?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 17 the person is the Government` MEANS
    IF `the person is lawfully performing executive functions of the Government under a law`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "The word \"Government\" includes any person lawfully performing executive functions of the Government under any law": a person who is not is not said to be outside the word

§§ `s 22A - "fault element" and "physical element"`

-- src:1534 | “Fault element” and “physical element”
-- src:1536 | 22A.—(1) A fault element of an offence refers to any state of mind, proof of which is
-- src:1537 | needed to establish liability under that offence, including but not limited to intention,
-- src:1538 | wilfulness, knowledge, rashness and negligence.
-- src:1542 | (2) A physical element of an offence refers to any fact, proof of which is needed to
-- src:1543 | establish liability under that offence, and that is not a fault element of that offence.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 22A (src:1534-1543)
DECLARE `Kind of element of an offence` IS ONE OF
    `a fault element`
    `a physical element`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 22A (src:1534-1543)
@export What kind of element of an offence a thing is under section 22A of the Penal Code 1871: a state of mind, proof of which is needed to establish liability, is a fault element (including but not limited to intention, wilfulness, knowledge, rashness and negligence); a fact, proof of which is needed, that is not a fault element, is a physical element; NOTHING where proof of it is not needed
GIVEN `it is a state of mind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a state of mind (intention, wilfulness, knowledge, rashness, negligence or another)? FALSE for a fact
      `proof of it is needed to establish liability under the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is proof of it needed to establish liability under that offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of element of an offence`
`s 22A the kind of element` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `proof of it is needed to establish liability under the offence` AND `it is a state of mind`
        THEN JUST `a fault element`
        IF `proof of it is needed to establish liability under the offence`
        THEN JUST `a physical element`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "refers to" a state of mind or a fact "proof of which is needed to establish liability"; what the section calls neither is not said to be outside the two

§§ `ss 29A and 29B - "writing" and "electronic record"`

-- src:1866 | “Writing”
-- src:1869 | 29A. The word “writing” includes any mode of representing or reproducing words,
-- src:1870 | figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 29A (src:1866-1870)
@export Whether a mode of representing words, figures, drawings or symbols is a "writing" under section 29A of the Penal Code 1871: the word includes any mode of representing or reproducing them in a visible form; NOTHING for any other mode (the list is not closed)
GIVEN `it is a mode of representing or reproducing words, figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a mode of representing or reproducing words, figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29A the mode is a writing` MEANS
    IF `it is a mode of representing or reproducing words, figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "The word \"writing\" includes any mode of representing or reproducing words, figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form": a mode that is not is not said to be outside the word

-- src:1872 | “Electronic record”
-- src:1874 | 29B. The expression “electronic record” has the same meaning as in the Electronic
-- src:1875 | Transactions Act 2010.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 29B (src:1872-1875)
@export Whether a thing is an "electronic record" under section 29B of the Penal Code 1871: it is if it is one within the Electronic Transactions Act 2010, the Act whose meaning the section adopts
GIVEN `the thing is an electronic record within the Electronic Transactions Act 2010` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the thing an "electronic record" as the Electronic Transactions Act 2010 defines it? That Act is not in the sources: the investigator answers it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B the thing is an electronic record` MEANS
    IF `the thing is an electronic record within the Electronic Transactions Act 2010`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The expression \"electronic record\" has the same meaning as in the Electronic Transactions Act 2010": what that Act does not call an electronic record the Code does not

§§ `s 32 - words referring to acts include illegal omissions`

-- src:1918 | Words referring to acts include illegal omissions
-- src:1920 | 32. In every part of this Code, except where a contrary intention appears from the
-- src:1921 | context, words which refer to acts done extend also to illegal omissions.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 32 (src:1918-1921)
@export Whether words which refer to acts done extend also to illegal omissions under section 32 of the Penal Code 1871: they do in every part of the Code, except where a contrary intention appears from the context; NOTHING for words that do not refer to acts done
GIVEN `the words refer to acts done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the words refer to acts done?
      `a contrary intention appears from the context` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a contrary intention appear from the context?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 32 the words extend to illegal omissions` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the words refer to acts done`)
        THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 32 speaks of words which refer to acts done
        IF `a contrary intention appears from the context`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except where a contrary intention appears from the context"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 33 - "act" and "omission"`

-- src:1923 | “Act” and “omission”
-- src:1925 | 33.—(1) The word “act” denotes as well a series of acts as a single act; the word
-- src:1926 | “omission” denotes as well a series of omissions as a single omission.
-- src:1930 | (2) To avoid doubt, where a person does a series of acts, one or more of which caused
-- src:1931 | a certain effect, that person is regarded to have caused that effect by that series of acts
-- src:1932 | even if it is not known which of the acts in that series caused that effect.
-- src:1934 | Illustration
-- src:1936 | A gives Z small doses of poison in Z’s food at different times over the course of a few days, with
-- src:1937 | intent to kill Z. Z dies from poisoning, although it is not known which of those doses (individually or
-- src:1938 | collectively) caused Z’s death. A has caused Z’s death by poisoning.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 33 (src:1923-1938)
DECLARE `Doing` IS ONE OF
    `a single act`
    `a series of acts`
    `a single omission`
    `a series of omissions`
    `something that is neither an act nor an omission`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 33(1) (src:1923-1938)
@export Whether the word "act" denotes a thing under section 33(1) of the Penal Code 1871: it denotes as well a series of acts as a single act; NOTHING for anything else
GIVEN d IS A Doing @desc What was done
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 33(1) the word "act" denotes the doing` MEANS
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `a single act` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a series of acts` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a single omission` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes as well a series of acts as a single act"; whether a single omission is an "act" it does not say (the next limb says what "omission" denotes)
    WHEN `a series of omissions` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see above
    WHEN `something that is neither an act nor an omission` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes" is not "means": a thing outside both is not said to be outside the word

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 33(1) (src:1923-1938)
@export Whether the word "omission" denotes a thing under section 33(1) of the Penal Code 1871: it denotes as well a series of omissions as a single omission; NOTHING for anything else
GIVEN d IS A Doing @desc What was done
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 33(1) the word "omission" denotes the doing` MEANS
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `a single omission` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a series of omissions` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a single act` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes as well a series of omissions as a single omission"; whether a single act is an "omission" it does not say
    WHEN `a series of acts` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see above
    WHEN `something that is neither an act nor an omission` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes" is not "means": a thing outside both is not said to be outside the word

-- Illustration: A gives Z small doses of poison in Z's food at different times over a few days, with intent to kill Z. Z dies from poisoning, although it is not known
-- which of those doses (individually or collectively) caused Z's death. "A has caused Z's death by poisoning."  Tested in pc-general-moved-tests.l4.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 33(2) (src:1923-1938)
@export Whether a person is regarded as having caused an effect by a series of acts under section 33(2) of the Penal Code 1871: where he does a series of acts, one or more of which caused the effect, he is, even if it is not known which of the acts caused it; NOTHING where the series did not cause the effect or is not a series
GIVEN `the person does a series of acts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person do a series of acts?
      `one or more of the acts caused the effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did one or more of the acts in the series cause the effect?
      `it is known which of the acts caused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it known which of the acts in the series caused the effect? The answer changes nothing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 33(2) the person is regarded as having caused the effect by the series of acts` MEANS
    IF `the person does a series of acts` AND `one or more of the acts caused the effect`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "To avoid doubt, where a person does a series of acts, one or more of which caused a certain effect, ...": it speaks only of a series, one or more of which caused the effect

§§ `s 36 - effect caused partly by act and partly by omission`

-- src:1957 | Effect caused partly by act and partly by omission
-- src:1959 | 36. Wherever the causing of a certain effect, or an attempt to cause that effect, by an
-- src:1960 | act or by an omission, is an offence, it is to be understood that the causing of that effect
-- src:1961 | partly by an act and partly by an omission is the same offence.
-- src:1963 | Illustration
-- src:1966 | A intentionally causes Z’s death, partly by illegally omitting to give Z food, and partly by beating Z.
-- src:1967 | A has committed murder.
-- Illustration: A intentionally causes Z's death, partly by illegally omitting to give Z food, and partly by beating Z.  "A has committed murder."
-- Tested in pc-general-moved-tests.l4 against `commits murder`.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 36 (src:1957-1967)
@export Whether causing an effect partly by an act and partly by an omission is the same offence under section 36 of the Penal Code 1871: where the causing of the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an act or by an omission is an offence, it is; NOTHING where neither is an offence
GIVEN `causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an act is an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an act an offence?
      `causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an omission is an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an omission an offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 36 causing the effect partly by an act and partly by an omission is the same offence` MEANS
    IF `causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an act is an offence` OR `causing the effect, or an attempt to cause it, by an omission is an offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 36 speaks of an effect whose causing by an act or by an omission is an offence; where neither is, it does not speak

§§ `s 38 - several persons engaged in the commission of a criminal act may be guilty of different offences`

-- src:1995 | Several persons engaged in the commission of a criminal act may be guilty of
-- src:1996 | different offences
-- src:1998 | 38. Where several persons are engaged or concerned in the commission of a criminal
-- src:1999 | act, they may be guilty of different offences by means of that act.
-- src:2001 | Illustration
-- src:2003 | A attacks Z under such circumstances of grave provocation that his killing of Z would be only
-- src:2004 | culpable homicide not amounting to murder. B, having ill will towards Z, and intending to kill him, and
-- src:2005 | not having been subject to the provocation, assists A in killing Z. Here, though A and B are both engaged
-- Illustration: A attacks Z under such circumstances of grave provocation that his killing of Z would be only culpable homicide not amounting to murder.  B, having ill will towards Z,
-- and intending to kill him, and not having been subject to the provocation, assists A in killing Z.  B is guilty of murder, and A is guilty only of culpable homicide.
-- Tested in pc-general-moved-tests.l4 against `commits murder` and `commits culpable homicide not amounting to murder`.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 38 (src:1995-2005)
@export Whether several persons engaged in the commission of a criminal act may be guilty of different offences by means of that act under section 38 of the Penal Code 1871: they may; NOTHING where not several persons are so engaged or concerned
GIVEN `several persons are engaged or concerned in the commission of a criminal act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are several persons engaged or concerned in the commission of a criminal act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 38 the persons may be guilty of different offences` MEANS
    IF `several persons are engaged or concerned in the commission of a criminal act`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 38 speaks of several persons engaged or concerned in a criminal act

§§ `s 42 - "obscene"`

-- src:2034 | “Obscene”
-- src:2036 | 42. The word “obscene”, in relation to any thing or matter, means any thing or matter
-- src:2037 | the effect of which is, if taken as a whole, such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons
-- src:2038 | who are likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter
-- src:2039 | contained or embodied in it.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 42 (src:2034-2039)
@export Whether a thing or matter is "obscene" under section 42 of the Penal Code 1871: it is if, taken as a whole, its effect is such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons who are likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter contained or embodied in it
GIVEN `taken as a whole, its effect is such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Taken as a whole, is the effect of the thing or matter such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons?
      `those persons are likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter contained or embodied in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the persons who might be depraved and corrupted likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter contained or embodied in the thing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 42 the thing or matter is obscene` MEANS
    IF `taken as a whole, its effect is such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons` AND `those persons are likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter contained or embodied in it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The word \"obscene\", in relation to any thing or matter, means any thing or matter the effect of which is, if taken as a whole, such as to tend to deprave and corrupt persons who are likely ... to read, see or hear the matter" (a closed definition)

§§ `ss 44A to 51 - "bodily injury", "life", "death", "animal", "vessel", "year" and "month", "section", "oath"`

-- src:2052 | “Bodily injury”
-- src:2054 | 44A. The words “bodily injury” denote as well a series of bodily injuries as a single
-- src:2055 | bodily injury.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 44A (src:2052-2055)
DECLARE `Bodily injuries` IS ONE OF
    `a single bodily injury`
    `a series of bodily injuries`
    `something that is not a bodily injury`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 44A (src:2052-2055)
@export Whether the words "bodily injury" denote a thing under section 44A of the Penal Code 1871: they denote as well a series of bodily injuries as a single bodily injury; NOTHING for anything else
GIVEN b IS A `Bodily injuries` @desc What was caused
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 44A the words "bodily injury" denote the harm` MEANS
    CONSIDER b
    WHEN `a single bodily injury` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `a series of bodily injuries` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `something that is not a bodily injury` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denote as well a series of bodily injuries as a single bodily injury": "denote" is not "means"

-- src:2060 | “Life”
-- src:2061 | 45. The word “life” denotes the life of a human being, unless the contrary appears
-- src:2063 | from the context.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 45 (src:2060-2063)
@export Whether the word "life" denotes the life in question under section 45 of the Penal Code 1871: it denotes the life of a human being, unless the contrary appears from the context; NOTHING where it is not the life of a human being
GIVEN `it is the life of a human being` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the life of a human being?
      `the contrary appears from the context` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contrary appear from the context?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 45 the word "life" denotes the life` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contrary appears from the context`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the contrary appears from the context"
        IF `it is the life of a human being`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes the life of a human being": whether the life of another being is "life" it does not say

-- src:2065 | “Death”
-- src:2066 | 46. The word “death” denotes the death of a human being, unless the contrary
-- src:2068 | appears from the context.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 46 (src:2065-2068)
@export Whether the word "death" denotes the death in question under section 46 of the Penal Code 1871: it denotes the death of a human being, unless the contrary appears from the context; NOTHING where it is not the death of a human being
GIVEN `it is the death of a human being` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the death of a human being?
      `the contrary appears from the context` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contrary appear from the context?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 46 the word "death" denotes the death` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contrary appears from the context`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the contrary appears from the context"
        IF `it is the death of a human being`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes the death of a human being": whether the death of another being is "death" it does not say

-- src:2070 | “Animal”
-- src:2071 | 47. The word “animal” denotes any living creature, other than a human being.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 47 (src:2070-2071)
@export Whether a creature is an "animal" under section 47 of the Penal Code 1871: the word denotes any living creature, other than a human being; a human being is not; NOTHING for anything that is not a living creature
GIVEN `it is a living creature` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a living creature?
      `it is a human being` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a human being?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47 the creature is an animal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it is a human being`
        THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any living creature, other than a human being"
        IF `it is a living creature`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes any living creature, other than a human being": a thing that is not a living creature is not said to be outside the word

-- src:2073 | “Vessel”
-- src:2074 | 48. The word “vessel” denotes anything made for the conveyance by water of human
-- src:2076 | beings, or of property.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 48 (src:2073-2076)
@export Whether a thing is a "vessel" under section 48 of the Penal Code 1871: the word denotes anything made for the conveyance by water of human beings, or of property; NOTHING for anything else
GIVEN `it is made for the conveyance by water of human beings or of property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it made for the conveyance by water of human beings, or of property?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 48 the thing is a vessel` MEANS
    IF `it is made for the conveyance by water of human beings or of property`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes anything made for the conveyance by water of human beings, or of property": "denotes" is not "means"

-- src:2078 | “Year” and “month”
-- src:2079 | 49. Wherever the word “year” or “month” is used, it is to be understood that the year
-- src:2081 | or the month is to be reckoned according to the Gregorian calendar.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 49 (src:2078-2081)
DECLARE Calendar IS ONE OF
    `the Gregorian calendar`
    `another calendar`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 49 (src:2078-2081)
@export Whether a year or a month is reckoned by the calendar under section 49 of the Penal Code 1871: wherever the word "year" or "month" is used, it is reckoned according to the Gregorian calendar
GIVEN c IS A Calendar @desc The calendar by which the year or the month is reckoned
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 49 the year or month is reckoned by the calendar` MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `the Gregorian calendar` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `another calendar` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "it is to be understood that the year or the month is to be reckoned according to the Gregorian calendar"

-- src:2083 | “Section”
-- src:2084 | 50. The word “section” denotes one of those portions of a Chapter of this Code which
-- src:2086 | are distinguished by prefixed numeral figures.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 50 (src:2083-2086)
@export Whether a portion of a Chapter is a "section" under section 50 of the Penal Code 1871: the word denotes one of those portions of a Chapter of the Code which are distinguished by prefixed numeral figures; NOTHING for anything else
GIVEN `it is a portion of a Chapter of this Code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a portion of a Chapter of the Penal Code 1871?
      `it is distinguished by prefixed numeral figures` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it distinguished by prefixed numeral figures?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 50 the portion is a section` MEANS
    IF `it is a portion of a Chapter of this Code` AND `it is distinguished by prefixed numeral figures`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "denotes one of those portions of a Chapter of this Code which are distinguished by prefixed numeral figures": "denotes" is not "means"

-- src:2088 | “Oath”
-- src:2089 | 51. The word “oath” includes a solemn affirmation substituted by law for an oath,
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 51 (src:2088-2089)
@export Whether a statement is an "oath" under section 51 of the Penal Code 1871: the word includes a solemn affirmation substituted by law for an oath, and any declaration required or authorised by law to be made before a public servant, or to be used for the purpose of proof, whether in a court of justice or not; NOTHING for anything else (the list is not closed)
GIVEN `it is a solemn affirmation substituted by law for an oath` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a solemn affirmation substituted by law for an oath?
      `it is a declaration required or authorised by law to be made before a public servant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a declaration required or authorised by law to be made before a public servant?
      `it is a declaration required or authorised by law to be used for the purpose of proof` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a declaration required or authorised by law to be used for the purpose of proof, whether in a court of justice or not?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 51 the statement is an oath` MEANS
    IF `it is a solemn affirmation substituted by law for an oath`
        OR `it is a declaration required or authorised by law to be made before a public servant`
        OR `it is a declaration required or authorised by law to be used for the purpose of proof`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "The word \"oath\" includes ...": a statement that is none of these is not said to be outside the word

§§ `ss 53 and 54 - punishments`

-- src:2112 | Explanation.—Caning shall be with a rattan.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 53 Explanation (src:2112-2112)
DECLARE `Instrument of caning` IS ONE OF
    `a rattan`
    `another instrument`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 53 Explanation (src:2112-2112)
@export Whether an instrument may be used for caning under the Explanation to section 53 of the Penal Code 1871: caning shall be with a rattan; NOTHING for another instrument (the Explanation does not say in so many words that another is forbidden: marginal reading, NOTES.md)
GIVEN i IS A `Instrument of caning` @desc The instrument the caning is to be done with
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 53 Explanation the instrument may be used for caning` MEANS
    CONSIDER i
    WHEN `a rattan` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `another instrument` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Caning shall be with a rattan" says what caning is done with; it does not say in its own words that caning with another instrument is not caning under the Code (marginal reading b: the other reading is JUST FALSE)

-- src:2114 | Imprisonment for life
-- src:2116 | 54. “Imprisonment for life”, in relation to any prescribed punishment under this Code
-- src:2117 | or any other written law, means imprisonment for the duration of a person’s natural life.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 54 (src:2114-2117)
@export Whether imprisonment for life, in relation to a prescribed punishment, is for the duration of the person's natural life under section 54 of the Penal Code 1871: it is, in relation to any prescribed punishment under the Code or any other written law; NOTHING where the punishment does not prescribe imprisonment for life
GIVEN p IS A Punishment @desc The punishment the offence's section prescribes
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 54 imprisonment for life is for the duration of the person's natural life` MEANS
    IF `prescribed at all` (p's `imprisonment for life`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 54 defines "imprisonment for life"; a punishment that does not prescribe it is not one the section speaks to

§§ `s 108B - abetment outside Singapore of an offence in Singapore`

-- src:3428 | Abetment outside Singapore of an offence in Singapore
-- src:3429 | 108B. A person abets an offence within the meaning of this Code who abets an
-- src:3431 | offence committed in Singapore notwithstanding that any or all of the acts constituting
-- src:3432 | the abetment were done outside Singapore.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 108B (src:3428-3432)
@export Whether a person abets an offence under section 108B of the Penal Code 1871: he does who abets an offence committed in Singapore, though any or all of the acts constituting the abetment were done outside Singapore; NOTHING where the offence abetted was not committed in Singapore
GIVEN `the person abets the offence within sections 107 and 108` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person abet the offence, as sections 107 and 108 (in pc-general-part.l4) read?
      `the offence abetted is committed in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence abetted one committed in Singapore?
      `any or all of the acts constituting the abetment were done outside Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were any or all of the acts constituting the abetment done outside Singapore? The answer changes nothing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 108B the person abets an offence` MEANS
    IF `the person abets the offence within sections 107 and 108` AND `the offence abetted is committed in Singapore`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 108B speaks of an offence committed in Singapore; it does not say that abetment of one committed elsewhere is not abetment (section 108A speaks to that)

§§ `s 26C(3), (4) and Explanation - intention`

-- src:1647 | (3) To avoid doubt, a person does not intend or foresee a result of his acts by reason
-- src:1648 | only of it being a natural and probable consequence of those acts.
-- src:1652 | (4) To avoid doubt, nothing in this section prevents a court from relying on a person’s
-- src:1653 | foresight that a certain effect was a probable consequence of his act as a basis to draw an
-- src:1654 | inference that the person caused that effect intentionally.
-- src:1656 | Explanation.—Intention is distinct from motive or desire, and a person may do an act or cause an
-- src:1657 | effect intentionally even if doing so was not his motive or he had no desire to do so.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26C (src:1647-1657)
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26C(3) (src:1647-1648)
@export Whether a person intends or foresees a result of his acts under section 26C(3) of the Penal Code 1871: he does not, by reason only of its being a natural and probable consequence of those acts; NOTHING where there is another ground
GIVEN `the only ground is that the result was a natural and probable consequence of the acts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only ground for saying that the person intended or foresaw the result that it was a natural and probable consequence of his acts?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 26C(3) the person intends or foresees the result` MEANS
    IF `the only ground is that the result was a natural and probable consequence of the acts`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "To avoid doubt, a person does not intend or foresee a result of his acts by reason only of it being a natural and probable consequence of those acts"
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "by reason only of": where there is another ground the subsection does not speak

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26C(4) (src:1652-1654)
@export Whether a court may rely on a person's foresight that an effect was a probable consequence of his act as a basis to draw an inference that he caused it intentionally, under section 26C(4) of the Penal Code 1871: nothing in the section prevents it; NOTHING where he did not so foresee
GIVEN `the person foresaw that the effect was a probable consequence of his act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person foresee that the effect was a probable consequence of his act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 26C(4) the court may rely on the foresight to infer intention` MEANS
    IF `the person foresaw that the effect was a probable consequence of his act`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection says that nothing in the section prevents reliance on foresight; where there was none it does not speak

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26C Explanation (src:1656-1657)
@export Whether the absence of motive or desire stops an act or effect from being intentional under the Explanation to section 26C of the Penal Code 1871: it does not; NOTHING where there was both motive and desire
GIVEN `doing so was not his motive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was doing the act or causing the effect not his motive?
      `he had no desire to do so` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had he no desire to do so?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 26C Explanation the absence of motive or desire stops the act being intentional` MEANS
    IF `doing so was not his motive` OR `he had no desire to do so`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person may do an act or cause an effect intentionally even if doing so was not his motive or he had no desire to do so"
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Explanation speaks of the absence of motive or desire

§§ `ss 79, 79A, 80 and 86 - who must prove the fault element, and what intoxication includes`

-- src:2578 | (2) To avoid doubt, where a person alleges a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact
-- src:2579 | that may negate the fault element of the offence that the person is charged with, the
-- src:2580 | prosecution must prove the fault element in order to establish liability under the offence.
-- src:2582 | Explanation 1.—Where the physical and fault elements of an offence are proven, and the accused
-- src:2583 | person proves the defence under subsection (1), the accused person is not guilty of that offence.
-- src:2585 | Explanation 2.—Where an alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact may negate the fault
-- src:2586 | element of an offence, the accused person does not need to rely on the defence of mistake. The
-- src:2587 | accused person may allege the mistake of fact or ignorance of the fact for the purpose of raising a
-- src:2588 | reasonable doubt that the accused person had the requisite fault element as a result of labouring under
-- src:2589 | the mistake of fact or ignorance of fact. The prosecution must prove the fault element in order to
-- src:2590 | establish liability under the offence.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79 (src:2578-2590)
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79(2) (src:2578-2580)
@export Whether the prosecution must prove the fault element where a person alleges a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact that may negate it, under section 79(2) of the Penal Code 1871: it must; NOTHING where no such mistake or ignorance is alleged, or it could not negate the fault element
GIVEN `the person alleges a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person allege a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact?
      `it may negate the fault element of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May it negate the fault element of the offence the person is charged with?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 79(2) the prosecution must prove the fault element` MEANS
    IF `the person alleges a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact` AND `it may negate the fault element of the offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "To avoid doubt, where a person alleges a mistake of fact ... that may negate the fault element": the subsection says what follows in those circumstances only

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79 Explanation 1 (src:2582-2583)
@export Whether the accused person is guilty where the physical and fault elements of an offence are proven and he proves the defence under section 79(1), under Explanation 1 to section 79 of the Penal Code 1871: he is not; NOTHING otherwise
GIVEN `the physical elements are proven` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the physical elements of the offence proven?
      `the fault elements are proven` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the fault elements of the offence proven?
      `the accused person proves the defence under section 79(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the accused person prove the defence under section 79(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 79 Explanation 1 the accused person is guilty` MEANS
    IF `the physical elements are proven` AND `the fault elements are proven` AND `the accused person proves the defence under section 79(1)`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the accused person is not guilty of that offence"
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Explanation 1 speaks of the case where the elements are proven and the defence is proven; it does not say whether the accused is guilty in any other case

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79 Explanation 2 (src:2585-2590)
@export Whether the accused person needs to rely on the defence of mistake where an alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact may negate the fault element, under Explanation 2 to section 79 of the Penal Code 1871: he does not; NOTHING where it could not negate the fault element
GIVEN `the alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact may negate the fault element of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact negate the fault element of the offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 79 Explanation 2 the accused person needs to rely on the defence of mistake` MEANS
    IF `the alleged mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact may negate the fault element of the offence`
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the accused person does not need to rely on the defence of mistake"
    ELSE NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Explanation 2 speaks of a mistake that may negate the fault element

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 79 Explanation 2 (src:2585-2590)
